<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haldimand Proclamation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1784">1784</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Haldimand Grant</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Haldimand Proclamation was a decree issued on 25 October 1784 by Frederick Haldimand, governor of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, granting the Six Nations of the Iroquois a tract of land six miles deep on each side of the Grand River in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>. It rewarded those who had fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a> and lost their homelands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Most of the tract was later sold or alienated, a loss still disputed today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haldimand-proclamation.jpg" alt="Haldimand Proclamation" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Creator:Thomas Ridout · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Ridout_map_of_Grand_River_Indian_Lands,_1821.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In November 1775, on the eve of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-brant">Joseph Brant</a> and Guy Johnson travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to obtain a promise that if the Iroquois fought on the British side they would receive a grant of land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. During and after the war American colonists confiscated the land and property of Loyalists. By early 1783 Brant had chosen the valley of the Grand River for settlement, and in 1784 Haldimand agreed.</p><p>The land first had to be bought from the Mississaugas of the Credit. On 22 May 1784 Colonel John Butler was sent to negotiate the purchase of about 3,000,000 acres between Lakes Huron, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> and Erie for £1180, a sale also known as the Between the Lakes Treaty. The proclamation followed three days after the Treaty of Fort Stanwix was signed between others of the Six Nations and the American government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Issued at the Castle of St Lewis at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, the proclamation recalled the early attachment of the Mohawk Indians to the King's cause and the loss of their settlement, and offered a safe retreat for them and others of the Five Nations who had lost their lands in the American states. It allowed them to settle on the banks of the Grand River, running into Lake Erie, six miles deep on each side from the lake to the head of the river, for them and their posterity to enjoy for ever.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Officials first read the grant as forbidding the Indians to lease or sell land to anyone but the government, while Brant held that Haldimand had promised freehold tenure like that of the Loyalist settlers. In 1793 Lieutenant Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-graves-simcoe">John Graves Simcoe</a> declared that they could not lease their land, since British subjects could lease only from British subjects; Brant saw the right to lease or sell as a test of Indian sovereignty.</p><p>The Crown held that the grant was for all the Six Nations, while Brant said it was for those who lived on the land. The Crown also contended that the grant was limited to land it had already bought, though the head of the river lay some thirty miles beyond that purchase. A map from the survey of 1791 was later believed lost or destroyed, and the Crown relied on Thomas Ridout's survey of 1821, which drew the northern boundary near present-day Elora. In 1819 John Brant, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-brant">Joseph Brant</a>, protested that the Six Nations held writings proving otherwise.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Haldimand Proclamation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Haldimand Proclamation</b> of 1784, Brant led many loyalist Haudenosaunee to the Grand River in present-day Ontario, where the Six Nations of the Grand River is now the most populous First Nations reserve in Canada.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
